    <h1>An AI agent<br /><span class="serif">on my website.</span></h1>
    <p class="story-lead">Turning a portfolio into a conversation, with clear boundaries around what the assistant knows and does.</p>
    <div class="project-links"><a class="text-link" href="#try-agent">Try the agent <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/tferrerd/tomasferrer.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View source on GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span><span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></div>
    <div class="story-meta"><div><span class="eyebrow">FOCUS</span><p>Conversational AI · Public profile</p></div><div><span class="eyebrow">BUILT WITH</span><p>Gemini · Node.js · Astro</p></div><div><span class="eyebrow">DELIVERY</span><p>Docker · Render · GitHub Pages</p></div></div>
    <div class="story-body">
      <h2>A different way to explore my work</h2>
      <p>My website brings together my experience, education, and selected projects. I wanted to give visitors another way to explore that material: asking a question. What did I work on at Cash App? How have I used forecasting? Which projects connect machine learning to a business decision?</p>
      <p>I built the assistant with Codex, working through the implementation, model choice, deployment, and live checks together. The result is the “Talk to my AI agent” button on this site. It answers in the first person, while the interface identifies it as my AI assistant.</p>

      <h2>Start with a small architecture</h2>
      <p>The website uses Astro to generate static pages hosted on GitHub Pages. Adding a conversation meant introducing a backend: somewhere to validate questions, assemble the model request, and keep the API key private.</p>
      <p>I kept that service small. A Node.js server receives the conversation, adds the foundational prompt and public profile context, calls Gemini, and returns an answer with links to the website’s source material. Docker packages the service, and Render hosts it. A GitHub Actions variable tells the frontend which backend URL to call.</p>
      <p>For this amount of information, I could include the public context directly in the request. The assistant has no external tools, browsing, or ability to act on my behalf. Its job is focused question answering about my professional background.</p>

      <h2>Make the knowledge boundary explicit</h2>
      <p>The knowledge is built from the website’s profile data and project pages. Keeping those sources alongside the site makes the content easy to inspect and update. Private job-search documents and credentials stay outside that knowledge.</p>
      <p>The foundational prompt defines the voice and scope: speak as Tom, answer questions related to my experience, refuse unrelated requests and prompt injections, and acknowledge missing information. It also asks the model to preserve dates and qualifications, distinguish employer outcomes from portfolio demonstrations, and avoid inventing achievements or commitments.</p>
      <p>Those instructions guide the model’s behavior. They still need to be tested, and they cannot guarantee that every future answer will be correct.</p>

      <h2>The deployment lesson was in the credentials</h2>
      <p>I initially wired the backend to OpenAI, then switched to Google Gemini when I looked more closely at ongoing cost. The deployed assistant uses Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite through a dedicated project on Google AI Studio’s Free tier, with the backend running on Render’s Free plan.</p>
      <p>Getting the key working exposed two separate issues. The original key allowed access to Google’s Agent Platform API, so Gemini requests were blocked. Saving a restriction for Gemini alone succeeded. The next request then revealed that the original project used prepaid billing and had no remaining credits.</p>
      <p>A separate AI Studio project, confirmed as Free tier, resolved that billing mismatch. Its key is stored as a secret on the backend. The browser only receives the service URL. That distinction matters: an enabled API, a permitted key, and a usable billing tier are separate pieces of a working deployment.</p>

      <h2>Test behavior as well as connectivity</h2>
      <p>I checked both the HTTP service and the model’s answers. Automated tests cover invalid requests, origin checks, usage limits, missing configuration, provider failures, and incomplete model responses. Live questions checked whether the assistant could describe my Cash App work, admit that it did not know my salary, and reject a request to invent a Stanford PhD and a role at Google.</p>
      <p>I also tested the first-person voice, an unrelated recipe request, and an attempt to expose the foundational prompt. After deployment, I verified a real answer through the production website and confirmed that a request from an unrelated origin was rejected.</p>
      <p>These were targeted checks of the deployed behavior. I have not measured visitor adoption, recruiter engagement, or a comprehensive accuracy rate.</p>

      <h2>The trade-offs of keeping it small</h2>
      <p>The service limits message size, conversation length, concurrent requests, and daily model calls. The daily counter lives in process memory and resets when the service restarts. Origin restrictions help control browser access, but direct callers can imitate an origin. Neither mechanism is a durable protection against sustained abuse.</p>
      <p>Free hosting also has a visible trade-off: the backend sleeps after inactivity, so the first answer can take about a minute. Gemini’s free usage is subject to quotas, and Google may use free-tier inputs and outputs to improve its products. The application does not save conversation transcripts; the chat history lasts only while the page remains open.</p>
      <p>The project gave me a working example of the decisions around an AI interface: what information to provide, what requests to accept, how to handle uncertainty, and how to verify the complete path from a visitor’s question to a useful answer.</p>
      <h2 id="try-agent">Try it here</h2>
      <p>Open “Talk to my AI agent” and ask about my work, skills, or projects. The prompt is designed to keep the conversation grounded in the professional information I have published on this site.</p>
